  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nLate Summer Heat Wave\nUpper-level ridging remains dominant across the Midwest, \nanchored by a robust 596 dam 500mb high centered over the mid- \nMississippi and Tennessee Valleys through Wednesday. This ridge \naxis is progged to shift toward the southeastern CONUS by late \nweek. An 850mb thermal ridge continues to run well above the \n90th percentile; with a 12Z morning sounding recording 23C, \nguidance continues to advertise 23-26C temperatures over the \nregion through Saturday.\n\nSurface temperatures will remain well above normal through the \nweekend. While dew points are expected to persist in the upper \n60s to lower 70s, we are seeing a seasonal decline in low-level \nmoisture contribution from evapotranspiration. This reduction, \ncoupled with deep mixing into a drier column, will help keep dew\npoints somewhat in check, though temperatures have a \nsignificant chance of reaching the 100-degree mark. NBM \nprobabilities for hitting triple digits are decent across \ncentral Illinois south of I-74 through Saturday, with the \nhighest probabilities (40-80%) occurring today and tomorrow. \nThese probabilities lower slightly Thursday through Saturday \n(30-60%) for the southwestern half of the CWA. Daily heat \nindices will generally range from 100-105 degrees through \nSaturday. A Heat Advisory remains in effect until Thursday and \nwill likely require an extension for at least portions of \ncentral Illinois.\n\nConvective chances remain largely confined to northern Illinois\nand the Great Lakes, closer to the jet stream, through the \nremainder of the work week. However, an isolated \nafternoon/evening pop-up storm cannot be ruled out as surface \ntemperatures approach convective thresholds daily. Given the \nlack of notable synoptic-scale forcing, the probability remains \nlow (less than 20%), though some CAMs, such as the RRFS, \nhighlight this potential. Should development occur, MLCAPE \nvalues of 2000-2500 J/kg and deep-layer shear of 25-30 kt \nWednesday and Thursday would support stronger storm activity.\n\nSlow Pattern starting this Weekend\nA closed upper low near Hudson Bay will dig toward New England \nby late week into the weekend, carving out a deep longwave \ntrough over the eastern CONUS. This will shunt the upper ridge \naxis back west across the central Great Plains, transitioning \ncentral Illinois into a northwest flow regime. At the surface, \nCanadian high pressure building across Quebec will drive a \nbackdoor cold front into Illinois, serving as the focus for \nprecipitation chances over the weekend. Temperatures will \nmoderate from the 90s ahead of the front to the lower-to-mid 80s\nin its wake. This transition appears slow, resulting in a \ntemperature gradient from the low 90s in the southern counties \nto the lower/mid 80s in the northern CWA by early next week.",
